          16745,
          16746,
          16747,
          16748,
          16751,
          16752,
          16758,
          16759,
          16760,
          16764
        ],
        "outcome": "not-feasible",
        "assessment": "Introduction to World Archaeology develops artefact description and technical drawing, Greek Archaeology studies material evidence for early democracy, and Heritage & Ancient Democracy uses objects, literature and history to interpret Athenian political life. These courses form a substantial starting cluster. The remaining history and arts courses broaden context but cannot develop excavation, finds processing, or specialist artefact analysis in sequence."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Heritage, museums and public memory",
        "generatingRegistryRows": [
          16729,
          16734,
          16737
        ],
        "supportingRegistryRows": [
          16749,
          16755,
          16756,
          16761,
          16763,
          16765
        ],
        "outcome": "not-feasible",
        "assessment": "African Art & Heritage and Modern Art & Modern Museums supply substantial material on collecting, interpretation and exhibition. Other arts, media and historical courses develop public communication and source analysis. A heritage curriculum could be coherent, but it would centre on art, institutions and cultural interpretation, with too little of the archaeological evidence and methods that define this degree."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Environment, landscapes and digital analysis",
        "generatingRegistryRows": [
          16733
        ],
        "supportingRegistryRows": [
          16750,
          16753,
          16754,
          16757,
          16762
        ],
        "outcome": "not-feasible",
        "assessment": "Earth Observation and Spatial Planning teach GIS and spatial methods. Dynamic Earth teaches geological stratigraphy and field observation, and Soils & Aquifers adds sampling and laboratory techniques. These are useful transferable methods. They lack a progression that applies those techniques to archaeological deposits, sites, artefacts and finds documentation."
      }
    ],
